# Feature-based folder structure

- Organize feature-specific code together under `pages/<feature>/`.
- Keep components, hooks, tests, types, constants, enums, fakes, mocks and utilities inside their owning feature unless they have demonstrated cross-feature reuse. 
- Place reusable UI and hooks in `common/components/` and `common/hooks/`; place shared pure helpers in `common/utils/` and app-wide constants in `common/constants/`. 
The same logic MUST be done for other types of folders: mocks, fakes, interfaces, types, constants, etc.
- Within a feature, use `components/` for smaller feature-only UI components and `hooks/` for feature-specific hooks. Avoid unnecessary nesting; usually no more than ~3 levels below the feature root.
- Keep external integration setup in `config/` (for example, analytics, Firebase, or GraphQL client providers).
- Ignore files in `client/src/components/eleks-ui` since it is a separate lib.

# Tests

- Place tests alongside the owning component. Keep mocks and fakes for tests in the appropriate folder.

## Preferable layout:

```text
client/src/
├── common/
│   ├── components/
│   ├── hooks/
│   ├── types/
│   ├── utils/
│   ├── constants/
│   ├── interfaces/
│   └── ...
├── config/
└── pages/
    └── RepositoriesList/
        ├── components/
        │   └── RepositoryListItem/
        │       ├── RepositoryListItem.test.tsx
        │       ├── RepositoryListItem.tsx
        ├── hooks/
        │   └── useRepositories.ts
        ├── constants/
        │   └── RepositoriesList.ts
        ├── utils/
        │   └── RepositoriesList.ts
        ├── mocks/
        │   └── RepositoriesList.ts
        ├── fakes/
        │   └── RepositoriesList.ts
        ├── ...
        ├── RepositoriesList.test.tsx
        ├── RepositoriesList.tsx
        └── index.ts
    └── ...
```

# Before finishing a client-code change, verify:
1. Is each file located with the feature that owns it?
2. Can you easily find the new file and related files looking into the structure?